{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.653,
            0.047,
            0.846,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Arithmetic Expressions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.088,
            0.5,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": "Writing it as sum of terms gives:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.406,
            0.11,
            0.59,
            0.15
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.159,
            0.551,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": "This expression means 24 more than $2 \\times 4 3$ . But, we want an expression which means twice or double of $4 3 \\pm 2 4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.215,
            0.551,
            0.251
          ],
          "content": "We can make use of brackets to write such an expression:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.251,
            0.413,
            0.268
          ],
          "content": "2 \\times ( 4 3 \\pm 2 4 ) .",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.27,
            0.552,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "So, we can say that together they have to pay $2 \\times ( 4 3 \\pm 2 4 )$ . This is also the same as paying for two vegetable cutlets and two rasgullas:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.343,
            0.425,
            0.36
          ],
          "content": "2 \\times 4 3 + 2 \\times 2 4 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.361,
            0.262,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.385,
            0.495,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "2 \\times (43 + 24) = 2 \\times 43 + 2 \\times 24.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.577,
            0.15,
            0.901,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.413,
            0.848,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "? If another friend, Sangmu, joins them and orders the same items, what will be the expression for the total amount to be paid?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.462,
            0.848,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": "? **Example 16:** In the Republic Day parade, there are boy scouts and girl guides marching together. The scouts march in 4 rows with 5 scouts in each row. The guides march in 3 rows with 5 guides in each row (see the figure below). How many scouts and guides are marching in this parade?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.56,
            0.848,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": "The number of boy scouts marching is $4 \\times 5$ . The number of girl guides marching is $3 \\times 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.599,
            0.415,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "The total number of scouts and guides will be $4 \\times 5 + 3 \\times 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.653,
            0.416,
            0.818
          ],
          "content": "This can also be found by first finding the total number of rows, i.e., 4 + 3, and then multiplying their sum by the number of children in each row. Thus, the number of boys and girls can be found by $(4 + 3) \\times 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.437,
            0.596,
            0.877,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.819,
            0.415,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, $4 \\times 5 + 3 \\times 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.837,
            0.272,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "= ( 4 + 3 ) \\times 5 .",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.856,
            0.545,
            0.875
          ],
          "content": "Computing these expressions, we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.875,
            0.813,
            0.918
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_20.jpg"
        },
        {
